\item{searched_files}{The files the search will read, exactly as for \code{\link{sdrf_design}}: each must
  be named \strong{exactly} by some row. A search that read \code{X-calib.mzML} for an SDRF naming \code{X.raw}
  matches nothing; \code{\link{sdrf_restrict}} the SDRF first.}

\item{out}{A \strong{folder}, created when missing. mzLib writes MetaMorpheus's \code{TmtDesign.txt}
  (1-based) inside it, replacing one already there, but only when the design is valid.
  MetaMorpheus reads it only from the folder of the first spectra file.}

\item{timeout}{Seconds to allow, or \code{NULL} to wait indefinitely.
}

}
\description{
Read an isobaric (TMT, TMTpro, iTRAQ, DiLeu) experimental design out of an SDRF, one row per file and annotated channel, in MetaMorpheus's terms, or every reason it was refused.

Answers \strong{can this SDRF drive an isobaric quantification, and which sample is in which channel of
which file?} Calls mzLib's \code{SdrfIsobaricDesign.Read}. It runs MetaMorpheus's own checks on a TMT
design - one plex, fraction and technical replicate per file; a channel described once per plex;
no sample twice at one fraction in a plex - and \strong{refuses rather than repairs}, reporting every
failure at once.
}
\details{
\strong{It guesses nothing:} not the kit, because one kit's labels can be a subset of another's, and
not the plex, which you declare with exactly one of \code{plex_column}, \code{plex_file_name_pattern} and
\code{single_plex}. Giving none, or several, is mzLib's refusal.

Channel labels are read by mzLib's \code{SdrfIsobaricLabels}, the rule \code{\link{isobaric_kits}}'s
\code{sdrf_label} writes: for TMT10, \code{TMT131} (PRIDE's spelling of the last channel) names \code{131N}
(mzLib #1467); for TMT11, where \code{131N} and \code{131C} are both channels, it is still refused.

\strong{The numbers are 1-based, as the SDRF writes them.} \code{\link{sdrf_design}}'s are 0-based. That split
is mzLib's, and mzLibR converts neither.
}
\value{
An \code{mzlibr_sdrf_isobaric_design}. \code{is_valid} is \code{TRUE} when there are no refusals.
  \strong{A refusal is an answer, not an error}: \code{refusals} then lists every reason at once, in mzLib's
  words, and \code{records} has no rows.

  \code{kit} is the kit name exactly as you gave it, and \code{kit_type} mzLib's name for the kit it
  resolved to (\code{"TMT10"}, \code{"TMT18"}, \code{"iTRAQ4"}, ...), as \code{\link{isobaric_kits}} names it; both \code{NA}
  when no kit was given. \code{kit_channels} lists every channel of the kit in reporter m/z order (a
  channel here and absent from \code{records} is one the SDRF does not annotate), \code{NULL} without a kit.
  \code{plex_column}, \code{plex_file_name_pattern} and \code{single_plex} echo the plex source you gave, \code{NA}
  for the others; \code{plex_source} is where mzLib read the plex from, as its report prints it,
  \code{"(not declared)"} when no single source could be used. \code{plex_ids} is a named vector, plex name
  to its number, 1 to N in case-insensitive name order (the order MetaMorpheus numbers plexes
  in), empty when refused; \code{plex_count} counts the plexes, 0 when refused. \code{file_key_column} is
  the column files were keyed on - \code{comment[searched data file]} when the SDRF has it, else
  \code{comment[data file]} - or \code{NA} when it has neither. \code{condition_columns} lists the columns the
  condition was built from; \code{condition_columns_declared} and \code{searched_files_given} say whether
  you passed them. \code{file_count} counts the files in the design and \code{record_count} the rows of
  \code{records}, one per file and annotated channel; both 0 when refused. \code{notes} says what was
  dropped or filled on the way; \code{report} is mzLib's account of all of it, to print. \code{written} is a
  list (\code{path} of the \code{TmtDesign.txt}, \code{file_count}, \code{record_count}), or \code{NULL} when \code{out} was not
  given or the design was refused. \code{caveats} says what the design does and does not cover.

  \code{records} is a data.frame, files in SDRF row order, then each file's channels in reporter m/z
  order: \code{full_path} (the searched path you passed for the file, else the file name the SDRF
  gives); \code{plex}, as the declared source gives it; \code{plex_id}, the plex's number in \code{plex_ids};
  \code{sample_name}, the SDRF \code{source name} of the sample in the channel; \code{channel}, as the kit spells
  it (\code{"127N"}); \code{condition}, the condition columns' values joined with \code{_}; and
  \code{biological_replicate} (as written, never ranked), \code{fraction} (1 when the SDRF has no fraction
  column) and \code{technical_replicate} (1 when it has no technical-replicate column), \strong{all
  1-based}.

}
\section{Restrict first, then read the design}{


A search reads \code{X-calib.mzML} where the SDRF names the acquired \code{X.raw}, so \code{searched_files} of
the search's own names match nothing. \code{\link{sdrf_restrict}} joins the two and writes the searched
name into \code{comment[searched data file]}, and this function keys on that column when a document
has it.

}
